WEBVTT

00:00:00.095 --> 00:00:02.352
This is someone shopping with only a keyboard.

00:00:03.295 --> 00:00:07.242
They press Tab to move through the page… and the Add to cart button gets skipped.

00:00:07.317 --> 00:00:08.599
They can't buy anything.

00:00:08.795 --> 00:00:16.667
That's not rare. In 2026, 95.9 percent of the top million home pages had accessibility failures.

00:00:16.742 --> 00:00:19.814
And almost all of them come from the same six mistakes.

00:00:19.995 --> 00:00:21.283
So we built Curb Cut.

00:00:21.358 --> 00:00:26.521
It fixes those barriers from the outside, right in your browser, and proves every fix works.

00:00:26.728 --> 00:00:33.320
Step one. Kane, an AI testing agent, tries the task the way a disabled person would. It fails.

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:36.494
Kane tries again with a mouse. The same task works.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.809
So the website is broken, not the task.

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:43.240
Step two. An agent writes a small, safe fix. Just rules, no code.

00:00:43.428 --> 00:00:48.762
Step three. Kane runs the task again with the fix, and it passes. The recording is the proof.

00:00:48.962 --> 00:00:53.509
Step four. The extension shares the proven fix with everyone who visits that site.

00:00:53.695 --> 00:01:00.842
Some barriers are whole journeys. On mass.edu, a keyboard user reaches About, and the menu never opens.

00:01:01.028 --> 00:01:05.370
With Curb Cut, the same keys open the menu, and Enter takes you to the page.

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:11.494
For blind users, the most common barrier is a button with no name. A screen reader just says, button.

00:01:11.569 --> 00:01:17.429
On Munich Airport, Curb Cut names it: previous slide. Kane found it with the fix, and couldn't without it.

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:24.347
The most common failure of all is faint text. Curb Cut nudges the colour just enough to be readable, and measures it.

00:01:24.528 --> 00:01:33.750
And on pages we've never fixed, there's an experiment. Jev, a fast decision model from TypeSafe, names unlabelled buttons live, in about a second.

00:01:33.825 --> 00:01:39.009
On real sites, 13 of its 15 names were right. When it isn't sure, it stays quiet.

00:01:39.195 --> 00:01:43.931
Then we locked the code and pointed it at 2,050 websites it had never seen.

00:01:44.006 --> 00:01:50.427
It fixed 99 of 142 barriers on its own, including 51 of the 89 hard ones.

00:01:50.628 --> 00:01:57.946
On 400 more new sites, we tested the five other common failures. Curb Cut fixed 76 of 118.

00:01:58.128 --> 00:02:05.720
Kane checked random samples of those fixes, and proved 11 of 20, then 7 of 15. Where it couldn't, we say why.

00:02:05.795 --> 00:02:11.425
Today, 105 fixes are live in the extension. 61 of them proven by Kane.

00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:20.439
And every fix can go back to the site owner: the exact change, with Kane's recording as proof. When the website fixes itself, our patch retires.

00:02:20.628 --> 00:02:25.023
Curb Cut. Fixing the web for the people it shuts out. Verified by Kane.
